{{also|dominó|Domino}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|Dominoes}}
[[Image:Domino (PSF).png|thumb|Two domino blocks]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr}} ''[[domino#French|domino]]'' (1801) < {{etyl|ML.}} {{term|domino|lang=la}} < {{etyl|la}} {{term|dominus||lord, master}}; compare {{etyl|ML.}} {{term|dominicale||a kind of veil}}. The game is said to be so called from the black under surface or part of the pieces with which it is played.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{enPR|dŏ'mĭnō}}, {{IPA|/ˈdɒmɪnəʊ/}}, {{SAMPA|/"dQmIn@U/}}
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|dämənō}}, {{IPA|/dɑmənoʊ/}}, {{SAMPA|/dAm@noU/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|pl=dominos|pl2=dominoes}}

# {{games}} A [[rectangular]] [[block]] divided into two [[square]]s, each having 0 to 6 [[dot]]s or pips (as in [[dice]]), used in the [[game]] of [[dominoes]].
# {{politics}} A [[country]] that is expected to [[react]] to [[event]]s in a [[neighboring]] country, according to the [[domino effect]]
# A [[masquerade]] [[costume]] consisting of a [[hooded]] [[robe]] and a [[mask]] covering the upper part of the [[face]].
#* '''1749''', Henry Fielding, ''Tom Jones'', Folio Society 1973, p. 485:
#*: all the women were desirous of having the bundle immediately opened; which operation was at length performed by little Betsy, with the consent of Mr Jones: and the contents were found to be a '''domino''', a mask, and a masquerade ticket.
# The mask itself.
# The person wearing the costume.
# {{geometry}} A [[polyomino]] made up of two squares.

